

GETPASS(3)          UNIX Programmer's Manual          GETPASS(3)



NAME
     getpass - read a password

SYNOPSIS
     char *getpass(prompt)
     char *prompt;

DESCRIPTION
     Getpass reads a password from the file /dev/tty, or if that
     cannot be opened, from the standard input, after prompting
     with the null-terminated string prompt on the standard error
     and disabling echoing.  A pointer is returned to a
     null-terminated string of at most _PASSWORD_LEN (32)
     characters; the rest of the line is read and discarded.

     SIGINT is ignored while echo is off, so an interrupt cannot
     leave the terminal silent.  The terminal's flags and the
     caller's SIGINT disposition are restored before returning.

FILES
     /dev/tty

SEE ALSO
     login(1), passwd(1), su(1), crypt(3)

BUGS
     The return value points to static data whose content is
     overwritten by each call.

     The buffer holds 32 characters, where 2.11BSD's holds 8.



